doc ///
    Key
        K3FamilyRecipe
        k3Recipe
        (k3Recipe, HashTable)
        realizeK3Surface
        (realizeK3Surface, K3FamilyRecipe)
    Headline
        validate a K3 family recipe and construct its marked model
    Usage
        recipe = k3Recipe spec
        K = realizeK3Surface recipe
    Inputs
        spec:HashTable
            family and construction parameters
    Outputs
        recipe:K3FamilyRecipe
        K:K3Surface
    Description
        Text
            The family @TT "complete_intersection"@ requires @TT "d"@ to be 2, 3, or 4.
            The optional parameters @TT "seed"@ and @TT "characteristic"@
            default to 1 and 32003. @TT "special_quadric"@ defaults to false and can be true for
            $d=3$. Both the batch decoder and @TO k3Surface@ use this validation.
            The recipe stores the family, normalized parameters, coefficient field and degrees.
            Use @TO k3Families@ to see the other families.
        Example
            recipe = k3Recipe hashTable {"d" => 2, "seed" => 7, "characteristic" => 10007};
            recipe#"parameters"
            K = realizeK3Surface recipe;
            K#"grading"
            K#"markedIdeals"
        Text
            The realized model also stores the actual equations, ordered ambient coordinates, grading,
            polarization and marked ideals. For complete intersections this records the sublattice
            generated by the hyperplane class, whose square equals the degree of the surface.
            Determining the full Picard lattice requires additional geometric evidence.
///

doc ///
    Key
        MarkedDivisorLattice
        markedDivisorLattice
        (markedDivisorLattice, Ring, List, Matrix)
        DivisorClass
        divisorClass
        (divisorClass, MarkedDivisorLattice, List)
        divisorPairing
        (divisorPairing, DivisorClass, DivisorClass)
    Headline
        an even integral lattice with an ordered basis and divisor coordinates
    Usage
        L = markedDivisorLattice(k, names, G)
        D = divisorClass(L, coords)
        divisorPairing(D, E)
    Inputs
        k:Ring
            the field of definition
        names:List
            distinct basis names
        G:Matrix
            a symmetric, nondegenerate integer matrix with even diagonal entries
        coords:List
            one integer coordinate per basis element
        E:DivisorClass
    Outputs
        L:MarkedDivisorLattice
        D:DivisorClass
    Description
        Text
            A lattice records its field, ordered basis and Gram matrix. Creating it from data
            supplies a numerical form. Geometric evidence is needed to identify it with a
            sublattice of the Picard group of a particular surface.
            A divisor class records its parent lattice and coordinates.
            Pairing requires the same parent, even when two separately created lattices have
            identical Gram matrices and basis names.
        Example
            L = markedDivisorLattice(QQ, {"H", "C"}, matrix {{4, 1}, {1, -2}});
            D = divisorClass(L, {2, -1});
            E = divisorClass(L, {1, 3});
            divisorPairing(D, E)
///

doc ///
    Key
        MukaiClass
        mukaiClass
        (mukaiClass, ZZ, DivisorClass, ZZ)
        tensorByDivisor
        (tensorByDivisor, MukaiClass, DivisorClass)
        catalogueMukaiClass
        (catalogueMukaiClass, K3Surface, String)
    Headline
        Mukai classes with coordinates in a marked divisor lattice
    Usage
        v = mukaiClass(r, c, s)
        tensorByDivisor(v, D)
        catalogueMukaiClass(K, name)
    Inputs
        r:ZZ
        c:DivisorClass
        s:ZZ
            the third component, equal to the Euler characteristic minus rank on a K3
        D:DivisorClass
        K:K3Surface
        name:String
            a catalogue name
    Outputs
        v:MukaiClass
    Description
        Text
            On classes $(r,c,s)$ and $(r',c',s')$, @TO mukaiPairing@ gives
            $c\cdot c'-rs'-r's$ using the shared marked parent of the divisor classes.
            @TO dualVector@ negates the divisor component.
            Tensoring by the line bundle of $D$ gives
            $(r,c+rD,s+c\cdot D+rD^2/2)$ and uses the same marked parent.
            @TT "catalogueMukaiClass"@ reads the numerical class by name without constructing its sheaf.
            The list-based catalogue methods and degree-based pairing remain available for the rank-one study.
        Example
            K = k3Surface 2;
            H = K#"polarization";
            v = catalogueMukaiClass(K, "M_H");
            w = tensorByDivisor(v, H);
            mukaiPairing(v, w)
            (dualVector w)#"divisor"#"coordinates"
///
